// Testing/HarnessSelfTest.maxon — the gate over the SPEC HARNESS'S OWN REFUSALS.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A REFUSAL NOTHING RUNS IS A CLAIM, NOT A DOOR** — this project's own rule, and until this
// file the harness broke it in six places at once. `SpecParser` refuses an empty ```RequiredRuntime
// block, an empty section pin, and a pin or runtime block on a case that never links; `TargetPrinter`
// refuses a ```RequiredRuntime name nothing emits and one the fragment already shows; and
// `pinsAnyResult` refuses a case with a program and nothing to check. Every one of them was written,
// reviewed, and then run by NOTHING. A refusal that has never fired is a refusal nobody has seen work.
//
// ⚠ **AN ORDINARY SPEC CASE STRUCTURALLY CANNOT PIN THESE, WHICH IS WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND IS
// SHAPED THE WAY IT IS.** The three `SpecParser` refusals are `panic`s raised while the harness parses
// its OWN corpus, in the parent process, before a worker is spawned (`buildPlans`). A deliberately
// malformed spec committed to `specs-shv2/` would therefore abort the WHOLE SUITE rather than fail one
// case — the gate would take the suite down with it every single run.
//
// ⚠ **AND PINNING THE PANIC TEXT WOULD NOT DO EITHER.** A Maxon panic prints `panic at
// <file>:<line>: <message>`, so an expectation carrying the whole text embeds `SpecParser.maxon:807`
// and goes red on every edit that moves a line above it — a false-red generator wired to an unrelated
// property. What this file compares is a SUBSTRING of the message, chosen to name the refusal and to
// contain no file, no line and no stack frame.
//
// ⇒ **THE SHAPE IS THE RUNNER'S OWN, ONE LEVEL UP.** `runStagedTest` already proves a compiler
// diagnostic by SPAWNING the compiler and reading its exit code and stderr; this proves a HARNESS
// refusal by spawning the HARNESS, pointed at a fixture corpus of deliberately-malformed specs, and
// reading the same two things. Each fixture is its own directory holding one `.md`, because
// `buildPlans` parses every spec in a directory before any filter is applied — so a second malformed
// spec beside the first would never be reached, and the case that never ran would look exactly like
// the case that passed.
//
// ⚠ **THE CORPUS IS DELIBERATELY NOT UNDER `specs-shv2/`.** `specFilePaths` lists `*.md` directly
// under the spec directory, so a subdirectory there would be invisible TODAY — but "invisible because
// the walk does not recurse" is a property of the walk, and the whole point of these files is that
// they detonate if anything ever reads them as ordinary specs. Living under `maxon-shv2/Testing/`,
// they are not in the corpus at all, whatever the walk grows into.

// Where the fixture corpus lives, relative to the repo root — anchored at the working directory for
// the reason `LadderIndex` and `spec-test`'s own default spec directory are: the suite is invoked from
// the repo root, and a run from anywhere else has already failed to find `specs-shv2`.
let FixturesRelativeDir = "maxon-shv2/Testing/harness-fixtures"

// The corpus root's own name, which is ALSO the recursion guard: a fixture case is driven by spawning
// this harness at `<FixturesRelativeDir>/<case>`, and that sub-run must not run the self-test again.
// Derived from the path the sub-run was GIVEN rather than signalled by a private flag, because a flag
// is a second way of saying which directory this is and the directory already says it.
let FixturesDirName = "harness-fixtures"

let FixtureSpecExtension = ".md"

// The line a fixture carries to say which refusal it is about: `<!-- expect-refusal: <substring> -->`.
//
// ⭐ **IT LIVES IN THE FIXTURE, NOT IN A ROSTER HERE, AND THAT IS THE `LadderIndex` LESSON APPLIED.**
// A hand-maintained table of (case, expected text) beside a directory of cases is one fact written
// twice, and the half that drifts is always the table. The case SET is derived by listing the
// directory and the expectation is read out of the case itself, so adding a refusal to the harness is
// one new directory and nothing else.
//
// It sits ABOVE the fixture's `## Tests` heading, so `extractTests` never scans the line — the marker
// grammar it shares (`<!-- key: value -->`) is read by `markerValue`, the same reader every other
// marker in a spec goes through.
let ExpectRefusalMarker = "<!-- expect-refusal:"

// ⭐ **PROVE EVERY HARNESS REFUSAL STILL FIRES.** Called ONCE, by the spec suite's parent process,
// before a worker is spawned — the placement `requireLadderIndexComplete` and `requireRunnerAvailable`
// already use, and for their reason: this is a property of the TREE, not of any one test, so there is
// no test to fail and nothing downstream that could act on it. It PANICS, naming the fixture and what
// its sub-run actually did.
//
// `workerExe` is what a fixture sub-run IS (this harness — its parser's refusals are half of what is
// under test); `compilerExe` is forwarded as `--compiler=` so the other half — the printer's two
// refusals — is asked of the COMPILER UNDER TEST rather than of whatever compiler this harness happens
// to be. In-tree they are the same binary; under the Phase 1 gate they are not, and that is exactly
// the configuration in which asking the wrong one would be silent.
//
// ⚠ The sub-runs deliberately do NOT inherit the run's `--target`. What a fixture asks is whether the
// HARNESS refuses a malformed directive, which no target changes — and two of the fixtures reach a
// refusal in the IR printer, which a target emitting no Target IR (wasm32) never runs at all. Under a
// cross-target suite run, forwarding the target would turn those two into false reds about nothing.
export function requireHarnessRefusalsFire(workerExe FilePath, compilerExe FilePath, specDir FilePath)
	if isFixtureCaseDir(specDir) 'thisIsAFixtureSubRun'
		return
	end 'thisIsAFixtureSubRun'

	let root = try FilePath.from(FixturesRelativeDir) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: '{FixturesRelativeDir}' is not a valid path")
	let cases = try Directory.list(root) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not list {FixturesRelativeDir} — it holds the deliberately-malformed specs that prove the harness's own refusals still fire. Run the suite from the repo root.")

	// EVERY fixture is run and every failure is reported TOGETHER, rather than stopping at the first.
	// One edit routinely breaks several — `refuseVacuousDirective` is reached through three fences — and
	// a report naming one of them sends the reader back for another full sweep per remaining fixture.
	// (`LadderIndex` reports its three directions in one message for the same reason.)
	var checked = 0 as FixtureTally
	var broken = StringArray.create()
	for caseDir in cases 'eachCase'
		if not Directory.isDirectory(caseDir) 'notACase'
			continue
		end 'notACase'

		match checkFixture(workerExe, compilerExe: compilerExe, caseDir: caseDir) 'verdict'
			fired then break 'verdict'
			didNotFire(reason) then broken.push(reason)
		end 'verdict'
		checked = checked + 1
	end 'eachCase'

	// An EMPTY corpus would let every refusal go unproven while this function reported nothing at all —
	// the exact silence it exists to remove, one level up. `LadderIndex` refuses a missing directory for
	// the identical reason.
	if checked == 0 'noFixtures'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: {FixturesRelativeDir} holds no case directories, so not one harness refusal was proven to fire. Each refusal is a subdirectory containing one {FixtureSpecExtension} that trips it.")
	end 'noFixtures'

	if broken.isEmpty() 'everyRefusalFired'
		return
	end 'everyRefusalFired'

	// ⚠ `panic` takes a STRING LITERAL — a `let`-bound String is rejected outright — so the list is
	// built as text and INTERPOLATED. Same constraint `LadderIndex.requireLadderIndexComplete` documents.
	let report = brokenRefusalReport(broken)
	panic("HarnessSelfTest: {broken.count()} of {checked} harness refusals did not fire on the fixture written to trip them. A refusal nothing runs is a claim, not a door.{report}")
end 'requireHarnessRefusalsFire'

// One fixture's verdict. A union rather than an empty-String reason, so "the refusal fired" carries no
// vestigial text and there is no sentinel to re-derive.
union FixtureVerdict
	fired
	didNotFire(reason String)
end 'FixtureVerdict'

// How many fixture cases one sweep proved. Named because it is the count the empty-corpus refusal
// reads, not a loop counter.
typealias FixtureTally = int(0 to u64.max)

// The failing fixtures, one indented paragraph each.
function brokenRefusalReport(broken StringArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for reason in broken 'each'
		out.append("\n  {reason}")
	end 'each'
	return out.build()
end 'brokenRefusalReport'

// Is `specDir` one of the fixture case directories — i.e. is this process a fixture sub-run? See
// `FixturesDirName`. A path with no parent (the filesystem root) is not one, which is the only thing
// the failure arm can honestly say.
function isFixtureCaseDir(specDir FilePath) returns bool
	let parent = try specDir.parent() otherwise return false
	return parent.filename() == FixturesDirName
end 'isFixtureCaseDir'

// Run ONE fixture and require that the harness refused it, saying what the fixture says it will say.
//
// TWO conditions, and both are necessary. A NON-ZERO exit alone proves only that something went wrong
// — a fixture whose program failed to compile for an unrelated reason would satisfy it — so the
// message must also carry the refusal's own words. And the substring alone would not do either: it
// could appear in output from a run that nevertheless reported PASS.
//
// A fixture that could not be RUN AT ALL (a spawn failure, a missing or unmarked spec) panics on the
// spot instead of joining the report: that is the harness's instrument being broken rather than a
// refusal being gone, and the two must not read alike.
function checkFixture(workerExe FilePath, compilerExe FilePath, caseDir FilePath) returns FixtureVerdict
	let caseName = caseDir.filename()
	let expected = expectedRefusalIn(caseDir, caseName: caseName)

	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(SpecTestCommandName)
	argv.push(caseDir.toString())
	argv.push("{CompilerFlagPrefix}{compilerExe.toString()}")

	let run = try runProcess(Executable.path(workerExe), argv: argv) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not run the '{caseName}' fixture ({e.displayReason()}) — it proves a harness refusal still fires, and a refusal nothing runs is a claim")
	end 'spawnFailed'

	// BOTH streams: a `SpecParser` refusal panics the sub-run's parent onto stderr, while the two
	// printer refusals reach the reader as a FAILED TEST's reason on stdout. One rule reads either.
	let said = "{run.stdout}\n{run.stderr}"

	if run.exitCode == 0 'harnessAcceptedIt'
		return FixtureVerdict.didNotFire("'{caseName}' was ACCEPTED (exit 0). It is a deliberately-malformed spec, and the refusal that should have caught it — the one that says '{expected}' — did not fire. Either that refusal was removed, or this fixture no longer trips it.")
	end 'harnessAcceptedIt'

	if not said.contains(expected) 'wrongRefusal'
		return FixtureVerdict.didNotFire("'{caseName}' failed (exit {run.exitCode}) but for the WRONG REASON. Expected the output to contain '{expected}', and it said:\n{said}")
	end 'wrongRefusal'

	return FixtureVerdict.fired
end 'checkFixture'

// The substring `caseDir`'s one `.md` says its refusal must print — see `ExpectRefusalMarker`.
//
// Every failure here is the FIXTURE being wrong rather than the harness, and each is a panic for the
// reason the whole file is: a fixture that cannot say what it expects proves nothing, and a self-test
// that skipped it would be the silence this file removes.
function expectedRefusalIn(caseDir FilePath, caseName String) returns String
	let text = try File.readText(fixtureSpecIn(caseDir, caseName: caseName)) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not read the '{caseName}' fixture's spec file")

	for line in text.split("\n") 'eachLine'
		if line.contains(ExpectRefusalMarker) 'isTheMarker'
			let expected = markerValue(line)
			if expected.isEmpty() 'noValue'
				panic("HarnessSelfTest: the '{caseName}' fixture's '{ExpectRefusalMarker}' marker names no text. It must carry the part of the refusal's message that identifies it — and NOT the `<file>:<line>` a panic prints in front of it, which moves on every edit above it.")
			end 'noValue'
			return expected
		end 'isTheMarker'
	end 'eachLine'

	panic("HarnessSelfTest: the '{caseName}' fixture carries no '{ExpectRefusalMarker} … -->' line, so nothing says WHICH refusal it is about — and a fixture that merely fails proves only that something went wrong.")
end 'expectedRefusalIn'

// The one `.md` in a fixture case directory. EXACTLY one, because a case is one refusal: a second spec
// beside it would never be reached (`buildPlans` parses in listing order and the first refusal panics),
// so it would sit there looking like coverage it is not.
function fixtureSpecIn(caseDir FilePath, caseName String) returns FilePath
	let entries = try Directory.list(caseDir) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not list the '{caseName}' fixture directory")

	var specs = FilePathArray.create()
	for entry in entries 'eachEntry'
		if entry.fileExtension() == FixtureSpecExtension 'isSpec'
			specs.push(entry)
		end 'isSpec'
	end 'eachEntry'

	if specs.count() != 1 'notExactlyOne'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: the '{caseName}' fixture holds {specs.count()} '{FixtureSpecExtension}' files, and a case is ONE refusal. A second spec beside the first is never reached — the first one's refusal aborts the sub-run — so it would look like coverage it is not.")
	end 'notExactlyOne'

	return try specs.get(0) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: the '{caseName}' fixture's spec list is empty despite its count being 1")
end 'fixtureSpecIn'
